WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
SIERRA: Hey, everyone! Just a heads&nbsp;
up that this is our last episode of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:07.260
the season. ﻿We're going to be taking&nbsp;
a break to dive into new episodes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:11.160
we'll be back in the late-Spring. In the&nbsp;
meantime, keep an eye on your feed for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:16.440
extras and the Season 4 trailer. Until the,&nbsp;
thanks for listening - on with the show!

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:25.320
SIERRA: It can be tough to try and make lifestyle&nbsp;
choices that will help build a healthier world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.480
Over and over, we find out that one of our&nbsp;
habits is contributing to climate change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.300
inequality, or the destruction of earth's&nbsp;
biodiversity. Even habits we thought were healthy.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.520
Take seafood. For years we were told that&nbsp;
fish were an important part of a good diet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.800
But now we are learning that many of the fish&nbsp;
we are used to eating are threatened species.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:52.800
Seafood is the largest traded food commodity&nbsp;
in the world, and it plays an essential role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.800
in our global diet. For some people, fish are&nbsp;
a luxury, but in many of the world's poorest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:05.100
and fastest-growing populations, it's a matter of&nbsp;
survival. At the same time, relentless industrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:11.100
fishing methods are pushing many species to the&nbsp;
brink. If things don't change, our fishing habits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:17.280
could devastate ocean biodiversity and deplete a&nbsp;
resource that humanity needs in order to survive.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.140
Fish are in trouble. But the&nbsp;
solution may not be what you expect.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.640
I'm Gabrielle Sierra and this&nbsp;
is Why It Matters. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.160
will there always be plenty of fish in the sea?

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:38.384
BBC: We have massive and widespread overfishing

00:01:38.384 --> 00:01:41.040
Teen Kids News: 76 percent of the world fish now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.775
are fished at capacity or&nbsp;
overfished and collapsing.

00:01:43.775 --> 00:01:47.760
The France 24 Observers: In the last 50 years,&nbsp;
the earth has lost half of its marine life.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:53.040
BBC: We have completely destroyed the&nbsp;
natural balance of fish in the world's ocean.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.280
SIERRA: So, are we going to run out of fish?

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:04.680
KURUC: If the current way that humans are&nbsp;
fishing continues and the current practices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.540
remaining unchecked, that doesn't set&nbsp;
up a good scenario for our future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:17.220
Hi, my name is Michele Kuruc and I'm the vice&nbsp;
president for Ocean Policy at the World Wildlife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:25.260
Fund in the United States. The pressure, the&nbsp;
amount of fish, the frequency, the quantities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:33.000
that fishermen are taking out are really causing&nbsp;
many stocks to be in very bad shape in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.160
their future viability. I mean, fish is one of the&nbsp;
most heavily traded food commodities that we have.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:45.000
SIERRA: Fishing really is a big business. In 2018,&nbsp;
the global export market reached an estimated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.680
value of $164 billion dollars. Asia is the leading&nbsp;
region in the fishing industry, with China, South&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:59.340
Korea, Japan, Taiwan, and one outlier - Spain-&nbsp;
fielding the most vessels globally. The European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:04.820
Union, the United States, and Japan are the three&nbsp;
places that consume the most fish by volume.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:10.560
KURUC: And it's really valuable, many&nbsp;
different species are very high cost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:17.520
so there's a lot of profit to be made in&nbsp;
catching fish. We have these man-made causes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.620
these drivers, these threats that are caused&nbsp;
by just better technology, bigger boats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:30.900
better refrigeration, easier methods of transport.&nbsp;
And then we have some of the man-induced natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:36.780
factors like climate change, which are also&nbsp;
changing the water quality, the composition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:41.880
the acidification of the water that fish call&nbsp;
home. And some species can't adapt that well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:48.540
They move to different parts of the ocean&nbsp;
to have a more hospitable climate. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:52.760
then raises lots of political issues&nbsp;
about who gets to catch and how many.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:59.580
SIERRA: If you have ever gone fishing, you know&nbsp;
the drill—a rod, a line, a hook, some bait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:05.760
and maybe a boat. But modern-day industrial&nbsp;
fishing looks nothing like that. The boats in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.520
fishing fleets are essentially gigantic floating&nbsp;
factories. Inside they have all the machinery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:18.960
necessary to process, freeze, and package hundreds&nbsp;
of tons of fish per day. Guided to schools of fish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:24.600
by sonar and spotter planes, some of these&nbsp;
ships drag nets that are over a mile long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:29.760
snaring whole schools of fish that they are&nbsp;
after, and many that they aren't after, too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:36.540
Some of the methods are shocking. Take bottom&nbsp;
trawling, which involves dragging massive nets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:42.720
across the ocean floor in the hopes of sweeping&nbsp;
up fish. The process destroys coral and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:48.120
ocean-floor ecosystems that are essential&nbsp;
for all kinds of life, just for one catch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.040
As fish populations decline, these&nbsp;
fleets are having to travel further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.880
from land and drag their nets deeper&nbsp;
in order to reach their targets.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:03.180
BARANGE: When it comes to fisheries, there are&nbsp;
two things that are very important to know. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:08.940
about one in three fish stocks is overfished.&nbsp;
And that means that we are catching more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:15.300
the population is able to produce. My name is&nbsp;
Manuel Barange. I'm the director of Fisheries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:20.220
at the Food and Agriculture Organization of&nbsp;
the United Nations. And I'm also a professor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:26.040
at the University of Exeter in the UK. So&nbsp;
to have an analogy with banking, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:30.540
we are eating into the capital rather than just&nbsp;
using the interest. And that doesn't mean that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:33.780
are collapsing the population. It's just that&nbsp;
this is not sustainable. We need, actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:39.300
to change that. But we see a dichotomy of what&nbsp;
is happening between developed countries and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:44.280
developing countries. In developed countries, we&nbsp;
see increasingly an improvement in sustainability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:49.500
status. In developing countries, we see the&nbsp;
opposite. We see a decrease in sustainability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:53.880
increasing volume of problems.&nbsp;
And those are not easy to resolve.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:06:00.660
SIERRA: It doesn't sound good. One in three&nbsp;
fish stocks, a.k.a. the types of fish we eat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:05.340
are being destroyed faster than they can&nbsp;
reproduce. And many others are on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:10.620
edge. The incredible waste and destruction&nbsp;
of industrial fishing fleets are awful to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:15.780
contemplate. If these are the only options,&nbsp;
then maybe it's time to lay off the seafood.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:22.280
SIERRA: I mean, are we all going&nbsp;
to just have to stop eating fish?

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:27.840
BARANGE: No, we certainly should not stop eating&nbsp;
fish. And in fact, we should eat more fish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:33.420
But that does not mean that everything is&nbsp;
right and good in the world of fishing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:36.780
fisheries. You know, when I was born, there&nbsp;
were 3 billion people in the world. If I live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.620
a full life, by the time I die, there will&nbsp;
be 10 billion people. So in my generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:45.480
we've multiplied by more than a factor of three of&nbsp;
the number of people, and we all want to eat; we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:50.160
all need to eat, and we all have a right to eat.&nbsp;
And so, of course, there's a pressure on all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:55.620
food systems, and all food systems in the world,&nbsp;
be that land-based or ocean-based, have impacts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:59.340
All of them have impacts. Some of them are&nbsp;
manageable, and some of them are less manageable.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:06.480
KURUC: Well, you know, right now, about one-half&nbsp;
of the world's population is dependent upon fish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:11.940
for about 20 percent of their protein, so we're&nbsp;
talking about over 3 billion people. So it's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:17.040
very important source, not only of food but of&nbsp;
nutrition, of livelihoods, of economic stability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:24.180
for communities that could have a whole cascade of&nbsp;
negative consequences if we stop eating fish or if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:31.500
we continue to have these dangerous practices. So&nbsp;
if, for instance, as you said, we stopped eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:38.520
fish, something has to take the place in humans'&nbsp;
diet, and there are many negative environmental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:45.420
consequences that would flow from that as well. So&nbsp;
it's not a simple yes/no, take one thing out, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:51.120
then everything else can operate as it does now.&nbsp;
It's an interconnected system. The ocean obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.920
provides not only fish but many other things that&nbsp;
we as humans need for the health of our planet.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:02.040
SIERRA: The solution can't just be to stop&nbsp;
eating fish altogether. And that's because food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:07.380
is a bit of a zero-sum game. If the world's&nbsp;
population doesn't get calories from fish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:12.180
they will have to get them from somewhere&nbsp;
else. Even vegetarian options, like soy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:17.100
can involve deforestation and other&nbsp;
environmental impacts. And in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:21.240
parts of the world, catching and eating&nbsp;
fish is a matter of day-to-day survival.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:26.700
BARANGE: When you look at the global level,&nbsp;
you know, fish production and fish consumption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:31.800
you see that we now consume twice the volume of&nbsp;
fish per capita that we used to in the 1960s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:37.920
But that is particularly the case in developing&nbsp;
countries. It is particularly the case in Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:41.760
in the Pacific Islands, in general,&nbsp;
in small island development states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:47.040
where there's very few other opportunities for&nbsp;
high-quality protein and very few opportunities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:54.480
for high-quality sources of micronutrients. Iron,&nbsp;
zinc, vitamins, all those are concentrated in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.900
fish. And those are particularly important&nbsp;
in pregnant women, children under five,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:01.440
so that is important to recognize, and&nbsp;
fish has a very significant role to play.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:07.260
SIERRA: Even in the developed world, where it can&nbsp;
be a luxury rather than a necessity, fish can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:13.200
an important part of a healthy diet. But to have&nbsp;
sustainable access to fish, we may need to ease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:18.140
pressure on the handful of species we are used&nbsp;
to eating and think about expanding our palettes.

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:23.760
BARANGE: I think that we humans like&nbsp;
to eat what we are used to eating. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:28.500
we know we go to the shops, and we have a shopping&nbsp;
list that tells us what is that we need. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:35.040
some people only want to eat cod or tuna. And we&nbsp;
don't have the flexibility of mind to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.240
that this is perhaps not the best thing to do. The&nbsp;
best thing to do is to eat the fish of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:44.760
whatever has been able to be caught that day&nbsp;
or that week by the fishermen in your area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:48.480
And part of the problem is that we don't&nbsp;
know how to cook different types of fish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:53.940
so there is an element of consuming education that&nbsp;
is quite important to make people see the greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:58.500
advantage and the fantastic possibilities that we&nbsp;
have when it comes to fish. I mean, compare it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:04.620
land-based animals. What do we have? Cows, pigs,&nbsp;
chickens, you know - you count with the fingers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:09.360
two hands. In the case of fish, you have about&nbsp;
600 species to choose. It's a great diversity.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:15.480
SIERRA: The problem isn’t really fishing&nbsp;
itself. It’s fishing a handful of species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:19.080
unsustainably. In the global&nbsp;
race to figure out how to feed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:23.760
billions of people without dooming the&nbsp;
environment, fish are actually one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:28.820
best tools we have. But we’re interacting&nbsp;
with them in a very short-sighted way.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:33.840
BARANGE: You know, when we talk about the oceans,&nbsp;
it's a single word, right? But it is a very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:38.160
very large space. We are talking about&nbsp;
70 percent of the surface of the planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:44.400
and over 90 percent of the habitable space in&nbsp;
the planet; and habitable not by us, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:50.520
habitable by organisms. So in terms of this&nbsp;
space that aquatic systems have, it is huge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:57.720
and yet we only produce about 2 percent of our&nbsp;
caloric intake in those systems. So we are really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:04.680
not using aquatic systems to the full. And if we&nbsp;
are to feed 10 billion people, we need to do that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:12.060
We can't just rely on land systems, which are&nbsp;
extremely stressed, as we all know. If we have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:16.800
increase, for example, agricultural production,&nbsp;
we need to start cutting forests. Who wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:22.500
do that? Or we want to intensify production by&nbsp;
using, for example, genetically modified strains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:27.420
and who wants to do that? So they are limits to&nbsp;
the capacity to produce food on land, and we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:31.680
not reached those limits in the water at all. And&nbsp;
furthermore, perhaps something that people don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:38.700
know. But fish are a very efficient converter of&nbsp;
feed into flesh. There's a particular ratio that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:44.340
we use very regularly in foul, and that is, how&nbsp;
much feed do you need to produce 1 kilogram of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:50.760
flesh of 1 organism. And in the case of fish,&nbsp;
that ratio is 1 to 1.3. In other words, we need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:58.740
1.3 kilograms of feed to produce 1 kilogram of&nbsp;
flesh in the fish. In chicken, it is 1.9. In pigs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:06.720
it is 4; in beef, it is 8, you need 8 kilograms&nbsp;
of feed for 1 kilogram of flesh. And this is for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:12.480
two reasons. First, because fish live in water,&nbsp;
and because of that, they have to invest less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:18.180
energy into creating bones. Because in water, the&nbsp;
effect of gravity is more controlled, they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:24.300
have to fight gravity in the same way that you or&nbsp;
I have to when we stand up. And furthermore, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:28.320
don't regulate their temperature. They actually&nbsp;
just keep the temperature of the water. So when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:34.260
it comes to looking at what are the advantages, or&nbsp;
what are the sources of food for the future, fish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:38.940
has so many boxes that you would tick. You have&nbsp;
to invest in fish for so many different reasons.

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:45.300
SIERRA: Compared to land, the ocean has a lot left&nbsp;
to offer. But the fish species we are targeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:52.620
are being fished to the brink. Bluefin tuna, red&nbsp;
snapper, halibut, swordfish, pollock, and even cod&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:59.040
are just some of the species under threat. What to&nbsp;
do? One solution is something called fish farming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:05.400
also known as aquaculture. Take a section of&nbsp;
ocean, close it off, grow fish, and let the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:10.980
rest remain untouched. But fish farming is not&nbsp;
as ideal in practice as it can seem at first.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:20.160
KURUC: So farmed seafood, which is usually called&nbsp;
aquaculture, makes up a really important part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:26.580
of our fish supply. It's been increasing in&nbsp;
recent years, and now it's almost half. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:32.160
on the one hand, it certainly relieves some of&nbsp;
the pressure on the wild fish stocks that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:38.340
trying to take out of the ocean. But we also&nbsp;
have to factor in what are some of the harms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:44.160
that aquaculture, farmed seafood, can cause?&nbsp;
And we have this conversion factor because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:49.020
farmed fish is going to take other things&nbsp;
out of the environment, like mangroves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:54.960
areas that are important nursery grounds for fish.&nbsp;
They serve lots of important functions for our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.960 --> 00:14:01.860
planet. There's an interconnectivity between both&nbsp;
farmed fish and wild-caught fish in the sense that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:08.460
if you have a fish farm, you need to feed those&nbsp;
fish. And currently, a lot of the fish food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:17.580
the fish meal is ground up wild-caught fish that&nbsp;
is fed to these fish that are on these farms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:24.600
So the fishing aspect, the wild-caught fishing&nbsp;
aspect, remains really important for both sectors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:29.400
both in the wild-caught as well as in the&nbsp;
farm sector. It's not like there's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:35.820
be an easy answer if we switch all of our fish&nbsp;
procurement to fish farming. The other thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:40.620
that I think is probably at least somewhat&nbsp;
interesting to know is that not all species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:46.020
have been successfully farmed. So the variety&nbsp;
of fish that we currently enjoy would go down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:50.960
dramatically because there are only certain types&nbsp;
of species that can be successfully farmed today.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:56.760
SIERRA: Aquaculture has its problems, but&nbsp;
the most significant pressure on threatened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:02.280
species remains traditional wild catches. And&nbsp;
to understand the kind of damage being done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:06.180
it helps to take a look at a species&nbsp;
that is not itself threatened...shrimp.

00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:18.060
KURUC: So, when shrimp are caught, typically,&nbsp;
there is about one pound of shrimp that's retained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:23.940
for nine pounds of other fish that are caught&nbsp;
in the same net, at the same time, that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:29.940
discarded because they're not part of the target&nbsp;
fishery. In other words, they're not shrimp. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:34.860
call them discards, and those are fish that are&nbsp;
typically dead, and they're thrown over the side.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:38.940
In the world of fishing, this is&nbsp;
sometimes referred to as “bycatch”.

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:44.220
KURUC: They're not utilized, at least, in&nbsp;
many countries. In some countries where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:49.260
they don't have the luxury of being able to&nbsp;
discard anything, all of it comes onshore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:54.720
but in most countries, that's not the case.&nbsp;
And it's not just other fish. Oftentimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:59.640
sea turtles get caught up in those nets&nbsp;
as well. Sea turtles need to breathe air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:04.740
they need to surface, they need to come up&nbsp;
pretty often, and those fishing nets that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:10.260
are fishing for shrimp typically stay down for&nbsp;
a long time. And it's one of the biggest hazards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:14.880
for our sea turtle population, and that's&nbsp;
the intersection with the shrimp fishery and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:20.160
trying to figure out ways to save turtles and&nbsp;
still preserve the fishing industry for shrimp.

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:24.240
SIERRA: If that’s not enough, there’s&nbsp;
another major problem with shrimp fishing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:30.060
it has been tied to severe human rights abuses,&nbsp;
including modern slavery aboard fishing vessels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:35.040
in Thailand. In fact, there are a host of&nbsp;
human rights concerns when it comes to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:40.500
global fishing industry, and the topic could be a&nbsp;
subject of its own episode. We encourage listeners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:44.880
to check out the resources in our show notes&nbsp;
to understand more about what’s going on there.

00:16:45.800 --> 00:16:53.270
SIERRA: And these unsustainable practices,&nbsp;
this is common across various types of fish?

00:16:53.270 --> 00:17:00.000
KURUC: In many, many types of fisheries, they're&nbsp;
looking for the ideal sized fish. Oftentimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.460
it means fish that fits on a dinner plate&nbsp;
or they're looking for certain condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:11.100
of the fish. They're looking for the fish that is&nbsp;
going to bring them the best price and everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:15.480
else. Regardless of whether they've caught&nbsp;
it or not, they'll throw it overboard. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:20.880
there is a very high amount of waste and&nbsp;
the unsustainability comes in. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:27.480
there are finite amounts of population. And so,&nbsp;
you can't only keep 10 percent and throw away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:32.760
90 percent and not expect that's going to have&nbsp;
an impact on the health of the fish populations.

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:36.360
SIERRA: It's wild to me because that's&nbsp;
not even the illegal fishing part.

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:36.592
KURUC: No. I know.

00:17:36.592 --> 00:17:38.460
SIERRA: That's the legal part.

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:40.980
KURUC: I know. I know.

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:45.060
SIERRA: The story of shrimp, and&nbsp;
the incredible waste of bycatch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:49.800
is just a window into the practices that&nbsp;
make humanity’s engagement with fish as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:54.840
food source so inefficient, and so&nbsp;
unsustainable. And as fish contend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:59.160
with ever-more relentless methods, other&nbsp;
threats are looming over their survival.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:05.580
BARANGE: There are many other activities in the&nbsp;
oceans that perhaps go a little bit under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:11.100
radar or not as in the radar as fisheries.&nbsp;
For example, coastal zone development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:18.540
the removal of mangroves and seagrasses for a&nbsp;
number of purposes, from mineral extraction to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.540 --> 00:18:25.440
building of marinas, to the expansion of bays, to&nbsp;
the dragging of harbors, a lot of those activities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:30.780
that perhaps one does not think so much in terms&nbsp;
of the impact. That is the first thing. Second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:36.960
of course, oil exploration and such activities&nbsp;
that do have significant impacts, most of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:42.600
activities take place within a narrow band from&nbsp;
the continental shelf, you know, they will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:48.600
largely within the first fifty kilometers or so&nbsp;
from the coast. That is the area of the oceans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:54.480
that is particularly impacted. And I'm including&nbsp;
here pollution issues, runoff from agriculture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:59.460
production, lots of nutrients that end up in&nbsp;
the sea, plastic pollution, and the like. Those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:05.280
areas are very, very highly impacted. When it&nbsp;
comes to high seas further away from the coast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:10.500
then the major impact really is climate change,&nbsp;
because the ocean plays a very significant role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:16.620
in regulating the climate. For example, 90 percent&nbsp;
of the additional heat that we have produced as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:23.100
result of global warming sits in the ocean. And a&nbsp;
quarter of the carbon emissions that we emit are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.100 --> 00:19:29.040
in the ocean. So the ocean has a very significant&nbsp;
role in climate regulation and also a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:35.820
impact. In fact, it is the geographical area that&nbsp;
will suffer the impacts of climate change most. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:41.040
when you think about temperature change, what&nbsp;
you will see is that organisms adapt to that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:46.500
So all fish tend to have an environmental&nbsp;
window that they feel comfortable in. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:50.400
that environmental window might be, say,&nbsp;
from seventeen to eighteen degrees. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:54.480
those seventeen to eighteen degrees move&nbsp;
physically because the water is warming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.480 --> 00:20:01.260
they will move with that water. And so we expect&nbsp;
a number of species to move towards the poles in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:06.480
both directions. So that's the first impact,&nbsp;
changes in distribution. There will be also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:11.520
changes in production. Some species will see a&nbsp;
decrease in production and others an increase in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:16.860
production. All that has huge implications for&nbsp;
food production in the ocean, at all levels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:21.300
if you can imagine, for example, a fisherman&nbsp;
suddenly going out to sea and not finding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:26.160
fish that they are used to fishing. And it's not&nbsp;
that easy to change to another species to fish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:30.720
because some of them require different boats,&nbsp;
different gear, and different technology and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:35.220
different expertise. And that goes all the way&nbsp;
up the food chain, all the way up to humans.

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:41.580
SIERRA: Fish don’t obey borders. The human&nbsp;
sphere is a lot thornier, and figuring out what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.580 --> 00:20:47.220
is and isn’t legal at sea can be confusing.&nbsp;
In order to understand how it works, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:52.560
turned to friend of the pod and CFR’s own Stewart&nbsp;
Patrick, who is an expert on global governance.

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:58.200
SIERRA: Who is in charge of the ocean?

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:02.160
PATRICK: Well, it's a really complicated&nbsp;
question. The oceans are massive, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:07.560
They cover 71 percent of the earth's surface and&nbsp;
about a third of that falls under the sovereign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:12.720
jurisdiction of particular nation-states. But&nbsp;
the rest of the oceans, the so-called high seas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:17.220
are really the quintessential global commons.&nbsp;
They belong to everybody and to nobody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:22.680
and they're governed by an incomplete patchwork of&nbsp;
treaties and bodies. Now, the closest thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:28.140
exists to a constitution for the world's oceans&nbsp;
is the UN Convention on the Law of the Seas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:32.880
But UNCLOS, to use its acronym, has a lot of&nbsp;
significant gaps, and that's particularly true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:37.980
when it comes to conservation and management&nbsp;
of natural resources, particularly fisheries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:43.620
Now the convention establishes special rights&nbsp;
for coastal states in their territorial waters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:47.700
but also in what are called exclusive&nbsp;
economic zones that extend about 200&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:52.620
nautical miles from shore. But beyond that,&nbsp;
there are a lot fewer rules. Now, in theory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:58.560
about 95 percent of the world's fisheries,&nbsp;
including the high seas, are governed by nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:04.380
twenty organizations called Regional Fisheries&nbsp;
Management Organizations. But the rules of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:08.880
bodies are limited to their own member states,&nbsp;
and they're often poorly enforced. Plus, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:13.740
only deal with a limited number of species, and&nbsp;
they're really uneven in their regulatory power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.740 --> 00:22:19.620
and their conservation mandate. Another issue is&nbsp;
that a lot of fish species are highly migratory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:24.420
and they travel back and forth between areas&nbsp;
of national jurisdiction and the high seas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:28.280
and nobody asks for their passports, but&nbsp;
you still have to manage these species.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:33.840
SIERRA: So what does this patchwork of&nbsp;
laws mean for the health of the oceans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:35.520
for fish, for biodiversity?

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:39.780
PATRICK: You know, the impact of&nbsp;
poor governance has been devastating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:46.680
and it's getting worse. Advances in technology&nbsp;
have really outpaced any kind of regulations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:52.140
stewardship of the oceans. They're permitting&nbsp;
this huge dramatic exploitation of the seas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.140 --> 00:22:56.040
and we're getting better at finding and&nbsp;
harvesting fish, but we're doing really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.040 --> 00:23:00.540
poorly in terms of conserving and managing&nbsp;
these resources that are so critical.

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:05.580
SIERRA: Is it possible to imagine a&nbsp;
new global system that could better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:08.940
regulate how humans interact&nbsp;
with the ocean and with fish?

00:23:10.140 --> 00:23:14.340
PATRICK: The best thing that could be hoped for&nbsp;
would be a Paris Agreement for the oceans in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:20.820
the countries of the world all came together and&nbsp;
pledged to do more to meet a global target. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:25.980
particular, to protect at least 30 percent of&nbsp;
the ocean in their territorial waters and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:31.800
on the high seas from human exploitation. This is&nbsp;
the target of the global campaign for nature. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:36.900
it's been endorsed by more than fifty countries.&nbsp;
But the hurdles to getting to this 30% target,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:42.720
particularly in the open ocean, are immense. The&nbsp;
best immediate step in the direction of protecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:48.240
the oceans would be to ratify a high seas&nbsp;
biodiversity convention, which is envisioned as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:53.280
an implementing agreement under the UN Convention&nbsp;
of the Law of the Sea. The high seas biodiversity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:58.500
convention would be designed to ensure that we can&nbsp;
serve and sustainably manage marine biodiversity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:04.620
in areas beyond national jurisdiction. And this&nbsp;
treaty would allow the identification of areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.620 --> 00:24:10.740
that are especially fragile and also important&nbsp;
ecologically, including for critical fish species.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:16.200
These areas would require special treatment.&nbsp;
Now negotiations over this draft treaty have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.200 --> 00:24:20.760
been going on for several years, and the fourth&nbsp;
and final stage of these negotiations is supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:26.580
to occur by the end of 2021. If this treaty were&nbsp;
ratified, it would mandate environmental impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:31.560
assessments of major commercial activities,&nbsp;
including deep-seabed mining. It would also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:36.660
allow countries to set up marine protected&nbsp;
areas and other area-based management tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:42.500
not only in their territorial waters but on the&nbsp;
high seas. It isn't perfect, but it would help.

00:24:42.500 --> 00:24:47.880
SIERRA: Coming to a global agreement on&nbsp;
how we can protect our shared oceans is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:52.320
perhaps the most important factor in&nbsp;
solving the problem. But there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:56.640
also a lot of local laws when it comes to&nbsp;
fishing. And they can have an impact too.

00:24:58.160 --> 00:25:04.700
SIERRA: What sort of regulations are there on&nbsp;
the fishing process? How strict is fish law?

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:07.140
KURUC: I love that term, fish law.

00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:10.980
SIERRA: I love it too. I think I might've&nbsp;
made it up, but you could have it.

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:14.460
KURUC: I know I've been doing this for a long&nbsp;
time, and I've never heard anybody call it fish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:21.840
law. So I think that's just great. There are&nbsp;
lots of aspects of fishing, licensing vessels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:28.080
operating vessels, who can do that, what kind&nbsp;
of a license do they need to have? What sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:32.820
restrictions are in place? Are there requirements&nbsp;
about almost all of that? The answer is yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.420 --> 00:25:38.940
there are, but what there isn't&nbsp;
is an equal system throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.940 --> 00:25:45.960
world that respects the rule of law that&nbsp;
enforces all of these requirements at an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:52.680
equally strict level. Obeying the law hasn't&nbsp;
necessarily been very well-respected either.

00:25:53.240 --> 00:26:00.180
SIERRA: Enforcing the law is hard enough within&nbsp;
a sovereign country on land. Enforcing it in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:05.700
vast expanse of an ocean system, without any&nbsp;
centralized authority, is nearly impossible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:10.860
Experts estimate that at least 30 percent&nbsp;
of the fish caught every year are caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:17.700
illegally. The perpetrators range from established&nbsp;
companies to transnational organized crime rings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:21.720
Within the realm of illegal fishing,&nbsp;
crimes range up and down the supply chain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.720 --> 00:26:26.160
including the targeting of protected&nbsp;
species, fishing in protected areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:32.700
fish-laundering, and mislabeling. One study&nbsp;
found that 36 percent of seafood products in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.700 --> 00:26:36.540
thirty countries had been mislabeled&nbsp;
by the time the consumer bought it.

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:43.200
KURUC: Measuring how much illegal activity&nbsp;
there is, is difficult. People who break the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:48.720
law try to hide what they do. In many cases in&nbsp;
fisheries, it's not hard. You look at two fish;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:54.420
you cannot tell from looking at them if&nbsp;
one was caught legally and one was caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:59.580
illegally. They look exactly the same.&nbsp;
It's quite a challenge to put in place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.580 --> 00:27:04.380
requirements and regulations that can&nbsp;
be easily enforced all over the world.

00:27:04.380 --> 00:27:09.900
SIERRA: So are there any examples&nbsp;
of regulations that have worked?

00:27:10.460 --> 00:27:15.360
KURUC: I'm glad you asked that question. I didn't&nbsp;
want this to be a story only of gloom and doom.

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:16.807
SIERRA: Me neither.

00:27:16.807 --> 00:27:20.520
KURUC: So the short answer is yes, and the region&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:26.400
that has really pioneered some of the most&nbsp;
successful programs is the European Union.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:32.280
And a little more than ten years ago, they&nbsp;
embarked upon the development of basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:32.280 --> 00:27:40.440
a two-tier program. It looks at the countries&nbsp;
that the EU is buying their fish from to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.440 --> 00:27:49.800
if they are adhering to appropriate standards for&nbsp;
enforcing their laws. Do they even have laws? Are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:49.800 --> 00:27:57.540
they basically a responsible trading partner when&nbsp;
it comes to how they supply fish to the EU? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.540 --> 00:28:05.400
they have used this program to sanction countries,&nbsp;
providing yellow cards, and in some cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:11.520
advancing to red cards. And if you get a red&nbsp;
card, just like in soccer, you're in big trouble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:11.520 --> 00:28:19.200
because the red card basically means that the&nbsp;
trade-in fish from your country is no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:25.140
going to be allowed into the lucrative EU market&nbsp;
until you basically clean up your act and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:31.860
EU has given you sort of a clean bill of health.&nbsp;
And so the reason that I feel that that has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.860 --> 00:28:39.960
so successful is because it harnesses the very&nbsp;
important power of trade and market leverage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:46.560
to try to get countries to do what they should&nbsp;
have been doing all along, to say, "If we want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:52.500
keep selling, if we want to keep our jobs, we want&nbsp;
to keep our trade, we have got to do better." It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:52.500 --> 00:28:57.840
has really changed in a relatively short period&nbsp;
of time, the activities of many, many, many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:57.840 --> 00:29:04.500
countries that have not historically been doing&nbsp;
very well. So, I would say that that's probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.500 --> 00:29:12.540
the most effective success story when it comes&nbsp;
to a new way to improve how we fish and to try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:18.300
make sure that some of the really bad things are&nbsp;
not rewarded by getting additional market share.

00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:23.520
SIERRA: The EU’s effort is worth looking&nbsp;
at because it doesn’t just address the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.520 --> 00:29:28.500
bloc’s own fishing practices. It uses their&nbsp;
economic clout to influence the behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:32.340
of other countries and regions.&nbsp;
With fish, as with so much else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.340 --> 00:29:36.600
money talks. And there are a lot of&nbsp;
other good ideas on the table too.

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:43.140
SIERRA: What reforms would you most like to see?&nbsp;
If fish as a food source is something that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:48.780
going to continue, that needs to continue,&nbsp;
you know, how do we get eating fish right?

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:55.860
KURUC: One of the biggest problems, and it remains&nbsp;
a problem today in many places, is the inability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:55.860 --> 00:30:05.700
to have transparent data about all aspects of&nbsp;
fishing operations and fish trade be available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:05.700 --> 00:30:12.540
so accountability and verification can be&nbsp;
achieved. We need to know who's out there fishing?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:18.360
How much fish are they taking? What kind of fish&nbsp;
are they taking? Is it really what they say it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.360 --> 00:30:24.840
is? We need lots of data. We need technology to&nbsp;
really be put to work on some of these issues. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.840 --> 00:30:31.380
if there was going to be one major change, that&nbsp;
would be it. So it takes the guesswork away that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:36.780
when you're a consumer, standing in your&nbsp;
grocery store saying, "Should I buy that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:42.060
piece of salmon for dinner tonight? Or is that&nbsp;
really a bad choice because I'm not really sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:42.060 --> 00:30:49.380
about it?" If you think that the government is&nbsp;
already making those evaluations for you, sadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:49.380 --> 00:30:54.780
in most countries, you would be wrong because&nbsp;
they're not. We shouldn't have to be wondering.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.780 --> 00:31:00.900
We shouldn't have to make a choice about our&nbsp;
food that's based on a lack of information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:07.560
There's one other thing. I mean, I talked&nbsp;
about this at a really high systems level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:15.420
but I will say that there's also another aspect to&nbsp;
this, and that's at the personal level. And what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:15.420 --> 00:31:20.340
can consumers, who really want to be responsible,&nbsp;
who care about the health of our planet, what can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:28.020
they do? There's a relatively simple thing. And&nbsp;
first and foremost, that is, speak up and ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:33.240
questions. And I don't mean that necessarily&nbsp;
in a political sense, although advocacy with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:33.240 --> 00:31:38.220
lawmakers is always great. But this is just much&nbsp;
more simple. When you're in the supermarket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:38.220 --> 00:31:42.180
or you go to the fish market, or you're in a&nbsp;
restaurant, and you're thinking about ordering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:42.180 --> 00:31:49.920
fish, you can ask the person that you're dealing&nbsp;
with, "Do you know if this is sustainably caught?"

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:56.100
BARANGE: You know sustainability is a bummer.&nbsp;
It is a really difficult thing to do. You are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:56.100 --> 00:32:01.740
always chasing your tail, right? So working&nbsp;
towards sustainable use is a difficult thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:01.740 --> 00:32:06.120
You have to look at every level, what is it&nbsp;
that is failing? You know, why is it that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.120 --> 00:32:10.620
are not making something so sustainable?&nbsp;
Now in the U.S., for example, NOAA, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:15.540
National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration&nbsp;
estimated that for every fish stock in the US,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:15.540 --> 00:32:22.200
to manage them properly, you need the order of $3&nbsp;
million a year. And so if you have twenty species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:27.660
you are looking, you know, north of $50 million&nbsp;
a year to manage those resources properly. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.660 --> 00:32:33.420
for the U.S., $50 million is not a lot. But for&nbsp;
some other countries, it's a lot of money. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.420 --> 00:32:38.640
so sometimes countries have different priorities,&nbsp;
and are not investing sufficient effort, money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.640 --> 00:32:44.580
infrastructure, people, scientists, to make those&nbsp;
resources sustainable. So there are many things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:51.660
that can be done, and very many that are not&nbsp;
working well. But the most important is to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:51.660 --> 00:32:56.760
decisions on the basis of science, number one,&nbsp;
and to have political support for those decisions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.360 --> 00:33:02.220
If you have those two, you can fix almost&nbsp;
anything. If you follow the science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:05.460
and you invest your political&nbsp;
will into following the science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:10.740
then fisheries and other food systems are very&nbsp;
sustainable and will feed us into the future.

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:16.380
SIERRA: The ocean belongs to all of&nbsp;
us. It touches every region of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:16.380 --> 00:33:23.340
earth. But its biodiversity is on the deep threat.&nbsp;
It’s not foolish to imagine the overstored ocean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.340 --> 00:33:29.460
the ocean that once again teams with fish&nbsp;
for millions and millions of years. Nor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:35.160
it’s foolish to imagine a sustainable ocean that&nbsp;
continues to provide food for ever-growing human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:35.160 --> 00:33:40.440
population. It’s not the one or the other.&nbsp;
We have the knowledge we need to do both.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:41.040 --> 00:33:44.400
The only question is whether&nbsp;
we will act on what we know.

